From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:28:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(web): close the H.264 decoders of surfaces that are gone,
 and bound them

WebCodecsH264Decoder opened a VideoDecoder for every surface a frame
named, and closed none until the session ended: nothing told it that a
surface was deleted, and a reset destroys them all. A server could name
as many surfaces as it liked (surface ids are 16 bits), with a small
CreateSurface and frame each, and every one kept a decoder, with its
memory and, where the platform has one, a hardware decoder: resources
the browser has for all of a page's sessions together. A server that
gives each new surface a new id left a decoder behind with every
surface it replaced.

ironrdp-egfx now tells which surfaces' streams ended: each H264Frame
names the surfaces deleted, created again or destroyed by a reset since
the frame before it (ended_surfaces), and the web client passes them on
to the decoder (endedSurfaces). The decoder of such a surface closes
once the frames given to it are out: a flush brings out what it still
holds, and a frame that does not come out then never will. Until then,
the stall check watches it as before.

At most 32 decoders are open at once. Servers stream H.264 to a surface
per monitor, a session has 16 monitors at most (MS-RDPBCGR 2.2.1.3.6),
and after a reset the decoders of the surfaces it destroyed may still be
finishing their frames while those of the new ones start. A frame for a
33rd surface fails the decoder, which ends the session as a decoder
error does.
